<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net8.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <PackageId>DeepSharp</PackageId>
    <Description>The best of three worlds: TensorFlow's way of describing a network, PyTorch's way of running it, and ML.NET's way of learning from a table. Deep learning in C#: the layer over the engines that already exist. Describe, train and use a network in C# while the arithmetic runs on .NET's own vector maths out of the box, or on libtorch through TorchSharp when the work gets bigger — swapping between them does not change a line of your model. And a table that a tree learns better than a network does not have to become one: the same prepared data is meant for ML.NET's trainers too. DeepSharp provides what an engine does not: the model's shape, the path your data takes into it, the training loop, the checkpoints and the charts.</Description>
    <PackageTags>deep-learning;machine-learning;neural-network;tensor;autograd;tensorflow;pytorch;torchsharp;keras;ml.net;dotnet</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="System.Numerics.Tensors" Version="10.*" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\..\LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\..\assets\icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
